Strategy Pattern vs. Visitor Pattern:

The Strategy Pattern and the Visitor Pattern are considered to be behavioral design patterns. Both of them
serve different purposes. The Strategy Pattern is used to define a bunch of algorithms and make them interchangeable.
It allows a client to choose the algorithm it wants to use at runtime which is a flexible trait of Strategy
Pattern. On the other hand, Visitor Pattern is used to separate algorithms and the objects on which they work on which
allows new operations to be added to a set of objects without modifying their classes. The Visitor Pattern is
useful when you have a stable class hierarchy and you want to add operations that depend on the type of objects
in the hierarchy. In our vehicle service part, it will be where different vehicles have different service charges.

The Strategy Pattern is more suitable for situations where the behavior of a single class needs to be diverse.
Like our emissions fee calculation in Activity 3, where the calculation logic can change based on the strategy chosen
(like one of these - OneEmissionsStrategy or TwoEmissionStrategy). Its also a good choice when behaviors are independent
of the object structure but are meant to vary.
On the other hand, the Visitor Pattern is ideal in scenarios where we have a well-defined set of classes and we need to
perform a series of related operations on them. It allows us to extend the behavior of these classes without modifying
their source code which is what we did in Activity 1 and 2 with the vehicle inspection service.

In my opinion, the Strategy Pattern makes more sense in scenarios where you need to change the behavior of a specific
class (like changing how we calculate service charges) and is often easier to implement when the behaviors are dynamic.
The Visitor Pattern is more useful when dealing with operations that need to be applied across an object structure and
you want to avoid changing the objects themselves, which makes it very powerful for operations like inspections or
applying a tax or fee structure across different vehicle types. Both patterns are valuable, but I would choose the
Strategy Pattern when flexibility in behavior is to be kept in mind, and the Visitor Pattern when operations need
to be extended across classes without changing the class structure itself.